      ]
    },
    "description": "The Feature Registry resolves a feature name to an immutable configuration record. The Registry Index checks its in-memory map, and the Remote Overlay Store may contain a newer revision for the same name. A lookup may legitimately find nothing: a feature can be disabled, not yet deployed, or intentionally absent from the overlay. The Feature Resolver needs to distinguish that ordinary absence from a malformed remote document, a timeout, and an authorization failure, since only the last three require diagnostics and retry behavior. When no record exists, the caller may apply a documented default and continue rendering the application. The current implementation returns null, which caused the Notification Center to dereference it and the Metrics Collector to count the absence as a successful zero-valued setting. Define the contract across Registry Index, Remote Overlay Store, Feature Resolver, Notification Center, and Metrics Collector so absence is explicit at every call site, while failures that require investigation still expose their reason and cannot be mistaken for a missing feature.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose the contract for a registry miss so callers must recognize ordinary absence without discarding or confusing actionable lookup failures.",
    "notes": "Option-maybe fits because a registry miss is legitimate absence with no reason while malformed, timeout, and authorization outcomes need distinct handling; result is the near-miss sibling and either supports the actionable-failure axis."
  },
